# Which SQLite engine the app opens, and what it falls back to.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and edit. Everything here is baked in at build time - Vite substitutes
# `import.meta.env` when it bundles - so changing it means rebuilding, not restarting.

# The engine tried first. The rest of the chain in src/app/storage.config.ts still follows it as
# fallback, so this reorders rather than restricts.
#
#   sqlite-wasm-opfs-sahpool     official @sqlite.org/sqlite-wasm, OPFS pool. Faster at writes and
#                                transactions, seeds ~1.8x quicker, and it is the SQLite team's own
#                                build. The default for those reasons.
#   wa-sqlite-access-handle-pool wa-sqlite, OPFS pool. Faster at joins and scans on measured
#                                hardware; a single-maintainer project.
#   wa-sqlite-opfs-async         wa-sqlite over OPFS without the pool, on the Asyncify build.
#   wa-sqlite-idb-batch-atomic   SQLite pages in IndexedDB. The slowest, and the only one that works
#                                without synchronous access handles - the option for a WebView
#                                between Chromium 86 and 108.
VITE_STORAGE_ENGINE=sqlite-wasm-opfs-sahpool

# PRAGMA profile. `safe` keeps SQLite's durability guarantee; `fast` trades it for speed by handing
# the flush decision to the OS, which is right for a benchmark and wrong for data a person would
# miss. Batched writes cost almost the same either way.
VITE_PRAGMA_PROFILE=safe
